{{also|Apina|apiña|apiñá|apiņa|apīņa}}
==Finnish==
{{wikipedia|Apinat|lang=fi}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|fi|gmq-osw-lat|apina}}, {{m|gmq-osw|apinia}} (compare {{cog|sv|apinja}}), from {{der|fi|non|apynja||female monkey}}. Compare {{cog|non|api}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{fi-p}}

===Noun===
{{fi-noun}}

# {{lb|fi|zoology}} [[simian]] {{gloss|ape, monkey or human; animal belonging to the infraorder Simiiformes within the order Primates}}
# [[monkey]] {{gloss|any nonhuman simian primate}}
#: {{ux|fi|Simpanssien tiedetään muodostavan laumoja ja metsästävän muita '''apinoita'''.|Chimpanzees are known to form bands to hunt and kill other '''monkeys'''.}}
# {{lb|fi|informal|derogatory}} [[ape]] {{gloss|uncivilized person}}

====Usage notes====
* In zoology, {{m|fi||apina}} means any simian, including humans. In everyday language, {{m|fi||apina}} is understood to refer to any simian except humans. In English "monkey" can be used similarly, but "ape" normally refers only to the most human-like simians of the clade Hominoidea, which the Finnish may call {{m|fi||ihmisapina}} but usually don't, using mere {{m|fi||apina}} instead.

